* R131. WORKSPACE OPEN LANDS IN THE CURRENT WORKSPACE (owner ruling 2026-08-29).
* Explicit-run until soaked. Self-bootstrapping in SANDBOX, self-erasing.
*
* WHAT CHANGED. A bare `WORKSPACE OPEN <dir>` used to derive a workspace name
* from the resolved directory LEAF, mint or adopt that workspace, switch into
* it, and open there -- so it walked OUT of the workspace the person was
* standing in. `AS <name>` is now the only naming form. R128's capability,
* "two directories into two workspaces", is unchanged; only the implicit name
* is withdrawn.
*
* WHY IT NEEDED A SPEC. The defect is INVISIBLE TO AREA CONTENT. Under both
* builds the opened table lands at the same slot with the same value in it,
* because the allocator is asked for a free area of whatever workspace is
* current -- and under the old build OPEN had just made that a different
* workspace. So an arm that reads the opened table reads GREEN on both. The
* discriminator has to ask WHICH WORKSPACE OWNS THE AREA, and the only thing
* in this language that answers that is a SCOPED SAVE: save the workspace the
* person made, reload it, and see how many areas came back. This is WAO_T5's
* shape, reused deliberately.
*
* FIXTURE DIRECTORIES. DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA, OJCB and OJCC must exist. They are
* EMPTY DIRECTORIES AND GIT DOES NOT TRACK THOSE, so a fresh clone will not
* have them -- the same standing fragility WAOA/WAOB/WAOC, PKA/PKB and
* RPCA/RPCB already carry. Stated rather than left to be discovered.
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA
ERASE OJCT1 CONFIRM
CREATE X64 OJCT1 (ID N(4), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "AAA"
CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCB
ERASE OJCT2 CONFIRM
CREATE X64 OJCT2 (ID N(4), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "BBB"
CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCC
ERASE OJCT3 CONFIRM
CREATE X64 OJCT3 (ID N(4), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "CCC"
CLOSE
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
* GUARDS. Each fixture read ON ITS OWN, before any of them is opened or added.
* Without these an arm reading the wrong MARK cannot tell a broken OPEN from a
* table that never carried the value -- WAO_G1 earned this paragraph by going
* red on both binaries when a SET PATH was written in the wrong order.
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA
USE OJCT1
? "OJC_G0_fixture_a_reads_AAA:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "AAA")
CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCB
USE OJCT2
? "OJC_G1_fixture_b_reads_BBB:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BBB")
CLOSE
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCC
USE OJCT3
? "OJC_G2_fixture_c_reads_CCC:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "CCC")
CLOSE
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
SET PATH DBF DBF
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE ARM. Make a workspace by hand, stand in it, put one table in it, then
* open a directory. R131 says the opened table joins OJCWS.
WORKSPACE NEW OJCWS
WORKSPACE SWITCH OJCWS
WORKSPACE ADD DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA/OJCT1.dbf
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/OJCB
* G3 -- the open happened at all, and the table is where a reader would look.
* GREEN ON BOTH BUILDS AND NOT A DISCRIMINATOR, stated rather than implied: a
* reader counting green arms would otherwise credit it with proving placement.
* It is here so that a run where OPEN did nothing at all cannot be mistaken
* for a run where OPEN landed in the wrong workspace.
SELECT 1
? "OJC_G3_the_open_landed_somewhere:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BBB")
* T1 -- THE DISCRIMINATOR. Save OJCWS, close everything, reload it, and add a
* third table. If OPEN joined OJCWS the posture carries TWO areas, the reload
* fills 0 and 1, the ADD lands at 2, and slot 1 still reads BBB. Under the old
* build OJCWS holds only OJCT1, the posture carries ONE area, the reload fills
* 0, the ADD lands at 1, and slot 1 reads CCC -- a printed .F., not a silence.
WORKSPACE SWITCH OJCWS
WORKSPACE SAVE ojcscope MEMO
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE LOAD ojcscope MEMO
WORKSPACE ADD DBF/SANDBOX/OJCC/OJCT3.dbf
SELECT 1
? "OJC_T1_opened_table_was_in_the_current_workspace:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BBB")
* T2 -- the placement half. WEAKER THAN T1 BY CONSTRUCTION: under the old
* build area 2 is never opened, so this arm may print NOTHING rather than .F.,
* which the harness sees as a missing marker rather than a red one. Kept
* because a missing marker is still a failure and because T2 green with T1
* green is the only combination that means what it says.
SELECT 2
? "OJC_T2_third_table_landed_past_both:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "CCC")
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* T3 -- THE COUNTERWEIGHT. A fix that made EVERY open join the current
* workspace would pass T1 and T2 and be exactly as wrong as the defect, so
* this arm asserts the opposite direction on the same fixtures: `AS <name>`
* must still put the opened table in a workspace of its OWN. Same scoped-save
* instrument, opposite expected answer -- OJCWS2 must come back carrying ONE
* area, so the ADD lands at 1 and slot 1 reads CCC.
* GREEN ON BOTH THE OLD AND THE NEW BUILD, which is the point: it does not
* discriminate the defect, it discriminates the OVERSHOOT.
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE NEW OJCWS2
WORKSPACE SWITCH OJCWS2
WORKSPACE ADD DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA/OJCT1.dbf
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/OJCB AS OJCASWS
WORKSPACE SWITCH OJCWS2
WORKSPACE SAVE ojcasscope MEMO
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE LOAD ojcasscope MEMO
WORKSPACE ADD DBF/SANDBOX/OJCC/OJCT3.dbf
SELECT 1
? "OJC_T3_AS_kept_the_open_out_of_the_current_workspace:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "CCC")
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* CLEANUP. Every name this spec mints is destroyed, so the next run mints
* fresh rather than adopting a live head. The superseded rows remain, which is
* the history D10.3 exists to keep.
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY OJCWS
WORKSPACE DESTROY OJCWS2
WORKSPACE DESTROY OJCASWS
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCA
ERASE OJCT1 CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCB
ERASE OJCT2 CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/OJCC
ERASE OJCT3 CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF
